<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José María Arguedas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José María Arguedas was a Peruvian novelist, poet and anthropologist who, raised among Quechua-speaking communities in the Andes, became the leading writer of indigenous Andean life in Spanish. His novels, including Deep Rivers (Los ríos profundos, 1958) and All the Bloods (Todas las sangres, 1964), portray the conflicts between Andean and Hispanic cultures. He also collected and translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> songs and myths, and he died by suicide in 1969.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-maria-arguedas.jpg" alt="José María Arguedas" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Se desconoce · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_Mar%C3%ADa_Arguedas_en_Chaclacayo,_Lima,_Per%C3%BA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
